  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 17,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.57,
            0.047,
            0.847,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A Tale of Three Intersecting Lines"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.31,
            0.086,
            0.695,
            0.211
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 1,
              "bbox": [
                0.31,
                0.086,
                0.695,
                0.211
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_image_1.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.229,
            0.828,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The blue line is the line with the least rightward bend that doesn’t meet the line "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\bar { \\mathcal { C } }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.272,
            0.846,
            0.309
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Visually, it is clear that the line that creates the smallest "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm { B }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " has to be the one parallel to l. Let us call this parallel line m."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.309,
            0.676,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can you tell the actual value of ∠B be in this case?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.328,
            0.561,
            0.346
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "["
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Hint:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Note that AB is the transversal.]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.346,
            0.846,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have seen that when two lines are parallel, the internal angles on the same side of the transversal add up to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 8 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . So "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm{B} = 140^{\\circ}."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.383,
            0.846,
            0.42
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, for what values of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm { B } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " does a triangle not exist? Does the length AB play any part here?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.419,
            0.848,
            0.477
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From the discussion above, it can be seen that the length AB does not play any part in deciding the existence of a triangle. We can say that a triangle does not exist when "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\angle \\mathrm { B }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is greater than or equal to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 4 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.483,
            0.294,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it Out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.509,
            0.848,
            0.567
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. For each of the following angles, find another angle for which a triangle is (a) possible, (b) not possible. Find at least two different angles for each category:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.57,
            0.293,
            0.59
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(a) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.594,
            0.293,
            0.614
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(b) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "7 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.618,
            0.293,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(c) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 4 ^ { \\circ }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.643,
            0.304,
            0.662
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(d) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 4 4 ^ { \\circ }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.667,
            0.848,
            0.802
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Determine which of the following pairs can be the angles of a triangle and which cannot: (a) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 5 ^ { \\circ } , 1 5 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (b) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "70^{\\circ},30^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (c) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "90^{\\circ} , 85^{\\circ}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (d) "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "5 0 ^ { \\circ } , 1 5 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.81,
            0.846,
            0.851
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Like the triangle inequality, can you form a rule that describes the two angles for which a triangle is possible?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.859,
            0.751,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can the sum of the two angles be used for framing this rule?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.806,
